# NestJS + TypeScript conventions

## Stack

- NestJS modular monolith, TypeScript, Node.js 22+, Jest (Nest default — not Vitest)
- GraphQL via Nest Apollo (schema-first); PostgreSQL + Prisma; pure scoring

## File structure (target)

Follow `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-28-weather-activity-ranking.md`:

- `src/main.ts` — API bootstrap; `src/worker.ts` — worker bootstrap (Task 7)
- `src/app.module.ts` / `src/app.worker.module.ts`
- Feature folders: `config/`, `scoring/`, `store/`, `open-meteo/`, `geocoding/`, `graphql/`, `refresh/`, `health/`
- Tests under `tests/unit/` and `tests/integration/` (adapt Jest config when adding them)

## Do

- Organize by **feature module** (`XxxModule` + colocated service/resolver/repository)
- Import feature modules from root `AppModule`; keep module boundaries clear
- Use `ConfigModule.forRoot({ isGlobal: true })` — do not re-import ConfigModule everywhere
- Keep **scoring pure**: algorithms unit-testable without Nest DI / Prisma / HTTP
- Prefer **strict** TypeScript (`strictNullChecks`, `noImplicitAny`); keep Nest CLI `tsconfig` defaults
- Use Jest (`describe` / `it` / `expect`); when adapting plan snippets that import `vitest`, convert to Jest

## Don't

- Don't call Open-Meteo on the warm GraphQL path (persist + compute-on-read)
- Don't wire GraphQL / Prisma / refresh worker ahead of their plan tasks
- Don't introduce microservices or Vitest for this submission
- Don't put Nest tutorials or style guides into `docs/01`–`05` (design docs stay product/system)

## Config env keys

`DATABASE_URL`, `PORT`, `REFRESH_INTERVAL_MS`, `REFRESH_CONCURRENCY`, `OPEN_METEO_TIMEOUT_MS`, `COLD_START_TIMEOUT_MS`, `STALE_AFTER_SECONDS`, `LOG_LEVEL`
